    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green: **58 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including the new `Cp46Tests` (2 tests). \n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. Changes:\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a `deleted` column (primitive `boolean`, defaulting to `false` so a newly created owner is never null/true), with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`.\n\n**Delete pipeline** \u2014 `DeleteOwner.java`: instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and re-saves, retaining the row. The DELETE endpoint still responds 204 (2xx) via the existing `RespondWithNoContent` step, and `GET /api/owners/{id}` continues to load and return the row afterward with `deleted: true`.\n\n**Create-time duplicate/identity checks** \u2014 both now skip flagged owners:\n- `RejectDuplicateIdentity.java` \u2014 the 409-blocking identity + household-duplicate scan `continue`s past deleted owners, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match is soft-deleted.\n- `AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 the soft-match flag likewise ignores deleted owners.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema (not `OwnerFields`, so it's output-only). The generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and MapStruct maps `owner.deleted \u2192 dto.deleted` by name, so it surfaces in GET responses.\n\n**Schema** \u2014 added the `deleted` column (`BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL`, `BIT(1)` for MySQL) to all four DB schemas (hsqldb used by the tests, plus h2/postgres/mysql for consistency). Existing seed inserts list columns explicitly, so the default applies cleanly.",
